 looks random to the extent a fair coin explains it better than a \"regular\" generator. There are two regular generators: a coin with an unknown tendency to switch or repeat, and a coin with an unknown bias towards heads or tails. Each one's unknown rate is averaged out under a uniform prior. The one distortion is that people believe a fair coin switches sides with probability q, which the model fits instead of fixing at ½. Each person also has their own decision sensitivity, drawn from a population distribution.\n\n**Why this answers the critique:** the critique's significant finding is that the current best model penalises perfect alternation too much. In this model the fitted q sets how much alternation counts as evidence for chance. It is weighed against the switching-coin generator, which explains a perfectly alternating sequence well. So whether such a sequence looks random or regular is decided by that fitted trade-off, not by a fixed penalty.\n\nThe model has three population parameters plus a person-specific sensitivity for each participant. It needs only two numbers per pair, both worked out from the raw sequences: the difference in switch counts, and the difference in how well the regular generators explain each sequence.\n\nThe self-check raised two warnings:\n- **Unreliable LOO estimate:** 5.2% of trials have a high Pareto k, above the 1% tolerance, so its ELPD estimate is flagged as unreliable (as are three of the models already in the set).\n- **Convergence not verified:** a single chain cannot report R-hat. The check also does not test novelty against the other models, and admission's full fit still has to meet the convergence limits.\n\nAll three files are in `/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment1/model_loop/iter_1/candidate_1/`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt`\n- `candidate.py`","ttft_ms":19060,"type":"result","duration_ms":269569,"uuid":"cf7efd5e-fcd1-463e-a3e2-d92f862fb59e","ttft_str